   "text": {
    "L1": "Water keeps moving through a cycle. The Sun's heat evaporates water from seas, rivers and soil.",
    "L2": "Plants also release water vapour from their leaves. This process is called transpiration.",
    "L3": "The vapour rises, cools and condenses into tiny droplets. These droplets form clouds.",
    "L4": "Rain, snow or hail falls from clouds. Some water runs over land, while some seeps into soil by infiltration.",
    "L5": "It moves through gaps in sand and gravel and cracks in rock. Water filling these spaces is groundwater, not a big underground lake.",
    "L6": "The water table is the top of the water-filled layer. A well below it can collect groundwater.",
    "L7": "Open soil, trees, ponds and recharge pits help water soak in. If pumping is greater than recharge, the water table falls.",
    "L8": "Now check yourself: which process names rainwater seeping down into the ground?"
   },
